honorable members mr browder  lawrence of the nobel peace prize and  the sarah of prize  distinguished guests  when innocent men and women get killed  nothing can bring them back nothing  can bring back sergey magnitsky  but there are things we can do to honor  his memory  we can hold accountable those who  violate the most basic human rights  we can act so that crimes like  magnitsky's death  do not happen again and this is why we  meet today  even if just virtually  europe has the power to make a  difference on the protection of human  rights  we have the power to sanction a crime  that is committed  outside our borders we have the power to  prevent  some crimes and not to forget the power  to make positive change happen  in some cases europe has already used  this power  two years ago for instance the european  union  sanctioned a group of burmese generals  they were responsible for the killing of  tens of  thousands of people from the rohingya  minority  so europe decided to freeze their assets  and to prevent them from entering the  european union  the day after we agreed on the sanctions  the government of myanmar removed those  generals from their posts  european sanctions had a clear impact  sanctions can make a difference as a  foreign policy tool  as a deterrent but most  importantly as a signal that europe does  act in the face of injustice  human rights must be sacred everywhere  from belarus to syria from myanmar to  venezuela  from russia to china  it shouldn't matter where the violation  took place  if there is a crime europe must have the  tools to act  and this is why a global eu human rights  sanctions regime was an important  announcement  in my state of the union address  and last month we have put forward our  proposal for the magnitsky act  which is now to be approved by european  governments  it can be a sea change for european  foreign affairs  up to now whenever we wanted to impose  new sanctions  we have to create a new legal framework  specific to the country and the  situation in question  and this can take a lot of precious time  and far too often we have seen that  simple decisions get stuck or delayed  for reasons having nothing to do with  human rights  so this has to change i want europe to  step up  i want europe to act quickly when human  rights are at stake  the european voice matters  and early and decisive action can  prevent further harm  so this is why we now propose a  framework for sanctions  that is global in scope the magnitsky  act  it is a strong tool  it is very often the case that those  responsible for human rights violations  are wealthy and powerful they benefit  from insecurity and undemocratic  structures in their  country but they themselves  like to take advantage of the  hospitality  of safe and democratic countries like in  the european union  and this is where we can sanction them  the sanctions regime includes the  possibility to impose travel bans  alongside with the freezing of financial  assets  human rights violations will have a high  cost for those who are responsible  it is now up to the member states of our  union to take the final decision  it is time to send a clear message to  the world  because when human rights are under  attack  i want europe to take clear positions  to defend human rights defenders to be  on the right side europe has the size  and the power to make a difference for  human rights  worldwide it is only up to us  to use that power thank you
 

